35	 * claim's reference, when it has one, is unique on its sheet without regard to case. A claim given no date
36	 * takes the sheet's today. A claim starts as DRAFT and moves through its statuses (see
37	 * {@link ClaimStatus}); a change its status does not allow is refused. Only a DRAFT can be edited:
38	 * to change an open claim, void it and add a new one. Settling a claim fixes its
39	 * due at the rate its person's grade has then for the claim's date; an open claim's due stays live.
40	 * A claim records who opened it, and that user cannot settle it (four eyes). A claim bound by its
41	 * person's last statement keeps the figure it had then, and settling it fixes that figure. A claim
42	 * belongs to the period its due-by falls in (fixed at creation: an edit, re-date or move leaves it).
43	 * No claim due inside a closed period can be added, edited or change status. No
44	 * claim can be added or edited to a date after its person's retirement date, nor after the from-date
45	 * and before the to-date of one of their past retirements. A clerk settles a claim
46	 * only when its figure is at or below the sheet's clerk limit (a claim with no figure is within any
47	 * limit); a clerk settling one above it makes it PENDING instead. A steward settles any claim, and
48	 * approves a PENDING claim (SETTLED, its due fixed then) or rejects it with a reason (back to OPEN);
49	 * the user who opened a claim cannot approve it. The user who last edited a claim (through its form or
50	 * its row's amount) can neither settle nor approve it, as its opener cannot. A PENDING claim is not bound by a statement: it
51	 * shows its live figure, and is bound again by the last statement once rejected. An overdue claim's due gains a late charge on its
52	 * figure (see {@link LateCharge}), and the floor is applied last: a claim's live due is never below
53	 * the sheet's floor. A statement binds only the rate figure, not the charge nor the floor, and settling fixes the charge into the settled due. A claim with
54	 * a negative amount is a correction: it is allowed only when it names, by number, a SETTLED claim
55	 * of the same person that it corrects. Its due has no floor and no late charge. A steward can move
56	 * a DRAFT, OPEN or PENDING claim to another person: it keeps its date, takes the next number (a
57	 * DRAFT, having none, gets none), loses any figure
58	 * a statement bound it to, and is listed as its new person's newest claim. A steward can re-settle
59	 * a SETTLED claim: its due is fixed again at the figure it would have today as an open claim bound
